{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>But Sarah your wife shall bear you a son.</b> One must wonder: did the Holy One, blessed be He, not already say to him, ״וְגַם נָתַתִּי לְךָ מִמֶּנָּה בֵּן״ (Genesis 17:16)? And the word ״נָתַתִּי״ — even though a promise of HaShem, blessed be He, is as though already given — nevertheless it appears that He used the language of the past, for He had already given Sarah the letter ה׳ which is the cause of procreation, as stated. And further: what does it mean when it says, ״הַלְּבֶן מֵאָה שָׁנָה יִוָּלֵד״ — was Abraham in doubt about the power of El, heaven forbid? For according to its plain sense, the implication is that this is not a real astonishment, and his friend would say of him, ״וְאִם שָׂרָה הֲבַת תִּשְׁעִים שָׁנָה תֵּלֵד״. And that which seems close to me in this matter is the following: we find the expression ״בֵּן״ even without birth, as it says regarding Moses our master, peace be upon him, ״וַיְהִי לָהּ לְבֵן״ (Exodus 2:10). So Abraham reasoned: since it says ״גַם נָתַתִּי לְךָ מִמֶּנָּה בֵּן״ and birth was not mentioned, surely His intention is that she would bring a foundling from the marketplace and raise him upon her knees, and it would be as though he were truly the son of both of them — for why would the Holy One, blessed be He, change the nature of things in a place where the words of HaShem can be explained as agreeing with nature? Therefore he said in language of astonishment, ״הַלְּבֶן מֵאָה שָׁנָה יִוָּלֵד״. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: ״אֲבָל שָׂרָה אִשְׁתְּךָ יוֹלֶדֶת בֵּן״ — an actual birth." "And that which He said, ״וְהָיְתָה לְגוֹיִם״ — in the plural — it is possible to explain that He alluded to him that in her merit all the barren women throughout the entire world were remembered, and through this it is as though many nations emerged from her — for from Sarah there emerged only Israel, one nation in the land, since Esau is not included, for it was already said, ״כִּי בְיִצְחָק יִקָּרֵא לְךָ זָרַע״ (Genesis 21:12)."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The quote 'ואם שרה הבת תשעים שנה תלד' is an allusion to Abraham's own words in Genesis 17:17; the phrase 'ויגיד עליו רעו' ('his friend would say of him') appears to reference the Torah's own description of Abraham's reaction, treating the verse itself as the 'friend's testimony.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:36.167975018Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}